[s0l0!-header][h1]New Lines[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The new line character [b]\n[/b] can be used as an alternative to [b]endl[/b].
Using a single cout statement with as many instances of \n as your program requires will print out [b]multiple lines of text[/b].

[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="cpp" codeId="173"]#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    cout << "Hello world! [b]\n[/b]";
    cout << "I love programming!";
    return 0;
}[/code]
[note]The [b]backslash[/b] (\) is called an [b]escape character[/b], and indicates a "special" character.[/note]
